    "sourceUrl": "https://apcentral.collegeboard.org/media/pdf/ap-computer-science-principles-course-and-exam-description.pdf",
    "officialTopicCodes": true,
    "designNotes": "CSP is organized into 5 Big Ideas rather than units; each Big Idea is stored as a 'unit' with the Big Idea's MC weight (CED Exam Weighting by Big Idea: 1 Creative Development 10-13%, 2 Data 17-22%, 3 Algorithms and Programming 30-35%, 4 Computer Systems and Networks 11-15%, 5 Impact of Computing 21-26%). Topic codes (1.1-5.6, 35 topics) from the CED topic tables; class periods are not stated per Big Idea and are left empty. Exam (read 2026-10-20): End-of-course exam Section I 70 MC, 120 min, 70% (57 single-select, 5 single-select on a computing-innovation reading passage, 8 multi-select with 2 answers; 4 options); Section II 30% = Create performance task (9+ hours in class: code, video, Personalized Project Reference) scored outside exam day, plus 2 written response questions (WR1, 2a, 2b, 2c) in 60 minutes using the student's Personalized Project Reference. No calculator concept; Exam Reference Sheet provided. Skills are the CED's practice skills 1.A-6.C (Practice 6 is not assessed in MC). Per-topic and per-sub-keyword skills are an ALTON editorial mapping (one skill per sub-keyword chosen by kind and content), not the CED's per-topic suggested skills. Sub-keywords, kinds, requires and estLessons are ALTON editorial designs, not CED content. Topic titles are the CED's where read from the CED course-at-a-glance; where the extracted text was truncated they are close paraphrases."
  },
